14:04:23 said  I finished the first version of your tree modeler, and Craft Anything's check passes: it builds, starts, and its self-test can orbit the camera, select a node, change a setting, add a node and open both editors.
14:04:23 said  - **Node graph:** add Trunk, Branch and Leaf nodes (they use the original's node icons) and they grow a 3D tree right away.
14:04:23 said  - **Properties panel:** on the left, filled in for whatever node, material or mesh you click. The starting settings come from the original's own node templates.
14:04:23 said  - **Material editor:** procedural bark and leaf textures, or load your own image. It has colour, opacity, bump, gloss and subsurface controls, plus a live preview.
14:04:23 said  - **Mesh editor:** you draw leaf cutout shapes over the texture, and it can generate one automatically and make simpler versions for distant detail levels.
14:04:23 said  - **Viewport:** perspective by default, with Front, Back, Left, Right, Top and Bottom views that switch to orthographic.
14:04:23 said  Not in this version yet:
14:04:23 said  - The original's Displacements tab.
14:04:23 said  - Its "Node" and "Freehand" editing modes (the buttons are there but greyed out).
14:04:23 said  - Six of its ten ways to place branches: I built Classic, Absolute, Interval and Phyllotaxy.
14:04:23 said  - Saving or exporting trees.
14:04:23 said  Two things are my best guess rather than copied from the original. I couldn't see inside its material and mesh editors without a tree loaded, so those layouts are mine. Some starting numbers, like how many branches and leaves appear, I chose by eye.
14:04:23 said  There are no tree presets. File › New tree starts you with just the Tree node, but the app opens with one default Trunk, Branch and Leaf so there's something on screen.
14:04:23 said  A good next request: send me a picture of the original's Material or Mesh editor so I can match it, or ask for the curve editors the original uses to shape a property along a branch.
